# Frontend (Furystack Shades)

The frontend uses Furystack Shades and `@furystack/inject`. Firmware C/C++ conventions do not apply in `frontend/`.

## Components

- Prefer `@furystack/shades-common-components` (`Button`, `Input`, `Alert`, `Loader`, theme helpers) before adding a custom control.
- Keep each Shade small. Split a screen into focused pieces such as `EditorToolbar` and `DeviceSignIn`.
- Put fetching, caching, and session logic in an injectable service. The Shade reads it with `injector.get(...)` and stays a view.

```typescript
export const DeviceAccess = defineService({
  name: "cos/DeviceAccess",
  lifetime: "singleton",
  factory: ({ onDispose }) => {
    const service = createDeviceAccess();
    onDispose(() => service[Symbol.dispose]());
    return service;
  },
});
```

## Pages

Load feature routes through `LazyFeature` and a dynamic `import()`. `App`, home, and not-found stay eager.

```tsx
const loadConsole = async () => {
  const { ConsolePage } = await import("../console/console-page.js");
  return <ConsolePage />;
};

"/console": {
  meta: { title: "Console" },
  component: () => <LazyFeature load={loadConsole} />,
},
```

## Theme

Color, spacing, type, and shape come from the active Shades theme (`ThemeProviderService`, `cssVariableTheme`). Use those tokens inside a Shade `css` block.

`frontend/src/style.css` is only the full-height viewport shell. New appearance belongs in the theme and in component `css`, using theme tokens.
